    "title": "Shell history file read for credentials",
    "desc": "A program read a shell or client history file - cat/grep/strings over ~/.bash_history, ~/.zsh_history, ~/.mysql_history, ~/.psql_history and friends. Command history is a rich source of credentials: passwords typed on a command line, connection strings, `-p` arguments and ssh targets all end up there, so sweeping history for them is a known credential-access step (MITRE T1552.003). history_tamper covers wiping history to cover tracks; this covers reading it to steal. A program reading the file is matched; the shell's own builtin `history` is not an exec and cannot be seen.",
    "analytic": {
      "type_id": 1,
      "type": "Rule",
      "name": "shell_history_read",
